 Solution $:$ There are three types of triangles we can make: The first type has two sides in the hexagon:
[asy]
path a=(0,0)..(6,0)..(6,3)..(2,3)..(2,6)..(0,6)..cycle;
draw(a,linewidth(1.4));
path b=(3,0)..(6,0)..(6,-3)..(2,-3)..(2,0)..cycle;
draw(b,linewidth(1.4));
draw((0,3)..(3,6)..(6,3),dotted);
draw((0,1.5)..(3,4.5)..(6,1.5),dotted);
dot((0,6)); dot((6,6)); dot((6,0)); dot((0,0));
dot((0,3)); dot((6,3)); dot((3,0)); dot((3,6));
label("Triangle",(3.12787348042,4.20674406046),NE);
[/asy]
There are ${ 7 \choose 2} -4 =9$ triangles of this type.<br/> \\ There are the second type of triangle which has members from three diagonals:<br/> [asy]
path a={(1,0)..(4,2)..(2,4)..(3,2)..(1,4)};
draw(a);
draw(shift(4,3)*a);
add(shift(4,3)*label("Type",(11.5,3.1)));
[/asy]
There are $7\times6$ of these triangles. \\ Finally, there are the third type of triangles. Three sides of the triangle are diagonals and one side is a side of the hexagon. They must be adjacent sides of the hexagon. In this case, there is one of these type of triangles.<br/> \\ Using the above results, there are $28+23=51$ triangles.<save this answer as $A1$</save> [/think] <answer> $A1$</answer>

